---
name: check-deployment
description: Check Fly.io deployment status for a CloudSurf app
allowed-tools: Bash
argument-hint: [app-name]
user-invocable: true
disable-model-invocation: false
---

**SCRIPT DELEGATION**: Run `./scripts/check-deploy.sh [app-name]` from the strategy repo root. Do not replicate this logic with AI — the script is the source of truth. Display its output to the user.

Check the deployment status for a CloudSurf app on Fly.io.

## App Registry

| Display Name | Fly App | Production URL |
|--------------|---------|----------------|
| surf-app (prod) | `surf-app` | https://app.surf |
| surf-app (develop) | `surf-app-develop` | https://surf-app-develop.fly.dev |
| WaveSite | `wavesite` | https://wave.site |
| SurfDoc | `tasksurf-app` | https://doc.surf |
| CloudSurf Website | `cloudsurf-website` | https://cloudsurf.com |
| SurfContext | `surfcontext` | https://surfcontext.org |
| Surf Server | `surf-server` | https://notesurf.io |

The script accepts: fly app name, display-name substring, or registry number (e.g. `surf-app`, `surf`, `1`).

## Deploy pipelines (context, not actions)

- **surf-app** is deployed by the `surf-build` worker pipeline: surf-app's own API exposes `build_jobs`, a gx10 Linux runner (`surf-build` daemon, `JobKind::RepoDeploy`) claims them, and runs `flyctl deploy --remote-only --strategy bluegreen` against `fly.production.toml` or `fly.develop.toml`. Source of truth for "is it live" is Fly itself — this skill checks Fly state, not the build-job queue.
- **Emergency fallback** for surf-app is GH Actions `bootstrap-deploy.yml` (`workflow_dispatch`, prod/dev choice). The canonical `deploy.yml` was deprecated 2026-05-17 in favor of the surf-build pipeline.
- The other apps (Wavesite, SurfDoc, surfcontext, etc.) deploy directly via `flyctl deploy` from their own repos or GH Actions; the script is agnostic to who pushed the release.

## What the script reports per app

- `fly status` — machine count + health
- `fly releases` (last 3) — version, status, when, who
- `GET <url>/healthz` — HTTP code

Requires `flyctl` installed and authenticated (`~/.fly/config.yml` access_token or `FLY_API_TOKEN`).

## When the script isn't enough

If you need to inspect a **failed/in-flight surf-build job** (e.g. the deploy never reached Fly because the worker rejected the job), query surf-app's API instead — `GET /api/builds` (user-scoped) or check the gx10 worker's systemd logs (`journalctl --user -u surf-build`). The skill does not cover this — it's strictly Fly-state.
